In modern MSTest (v3+), you can use **constructor injection** for `TestContext` and combine it with `[ClassInitialize]`/`[TestInitialize]` for sync setup and async lifecycle methods:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class MyTests
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;

    // Constructor injection for TestContext (MSTest v3+)
    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
    }

    [ClassInitialize]
    public static async Task ClassInit(TestContext context)
    {
        // One-time async setup for all tests in this class
        await SomeSharedResourceAsync();
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task TestInit()
    {
        // Per-test async setup
        await SetupPerTestStateAsync();
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public async Task MyTest()
    {
        _testContext.WriteLine("Running test...");
        // ...
    }

    [TestCleanup]
    public async Task TestCleanup()
    {
        await CleanupAsync();
    }

    [ClassCleanup]
    public static async Task ClassCleanup()
    {
        await DisposeSharedResourceAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- **Constructor injection** of `TestContext` is available in MSTest v3+. No need for the property-setter pattern.
- `[TestInitialize]` / `[TestCleanup]` can be `async Task` for per-test async setup/teardown.
- `[ClassInitialize]` / `[ClassCleanup]` are `static async Task` for one-time class-level work.
- The constructor runs before `[TestInitialize]` on each test; use the constructor for DI and simple field assignment, and `[TestInitialize]` for anything async or heavier.
